 "answer": "**If you mean today, Saturday, October 17:** KL’s latest reported Malaysian API readings were **160 at Batu Muda and 162 at Cheras around 10 p.m.**—in the unhealthy range. ([hazemonitor.com](https://hazemonitor.com/?utm_source=openai))\n\nThat’s **below the current school-closure threshold of API 200**, so it doesn’t automatically mean schools are closed. But Malaysia’s Education Minister has said parents may keep children home if they’re concerned, as long as they notify the school. Outdoor school activities should be suspended when API exceeds 100. ([bernama.com](https://www.bernama.com/en/general/news.php?id=2621391&utm_source=openai))\n\n**My practical take:** If your kids have asthma or another respiratory/heart condition, are feeling unwell, or would have to spend much time outdoors, keep them home if you can and let the school know. Otherwise, check the school’s notice and make sure they stay indoors with outdoor activities cancelled. If you mean the next school day, recheck the official APIMS reading and school updates that morning—the readings can change.",
